---
/**
 * Myth — Ravens have blue eyes
 * MYTH·11
 * Primary keyword: do ravens have blue eyes
 * Secondary: raven eye colour, raven eyes, baby raven eye colour
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title       = 'Do Ravens Have Blue Eyes? Raven Eye Colour Explained';
const description = 'Adult Common Ravens have dark brown eyes, not blue. However, juvenile ravens do have blue-grey eyes that gradually darken. This myth page explains the full story of raven eye colour across age classes.';
const path        = '/myths/ravens-have-blue-eyes/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',   url: '/' },
  { name: 'Myths',  url: '/myths/' },
  { name: 'Do Ravens Have Blue Eyes?', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'What colour are raven eyes?', a: 'Adult Common Ravens have dark brown eyes — often appearing almost black in field conditions. Juveniles (hatching to approximately 1 year old) have blue-grey eyes that gradually darken through the first year of life. By the time a raven reaches full adult plumage (typically 2–3 years old), the eyes are uniformly dark brown. The blue-grey juvenile eye colour is the likely source of "blue-eyed raven" claims.' },
  { q: 'Do baby ravens have blue eyes?', a: 'Yes. Nestling and juvenile ravens have blue-grey eyes, which is typical of many corvid species. The blue-grey colour is caused by the iris lacking the full melanin pigmentation that develops with age. As the bird matures through its first year, the iris darkens progressively through grey-brown to the full dark brown of adults.' },
  { q: 'Which raven species might have lighter eyes?', a: 'The Standard eye colour for adult ravens in all 10 species described on this site is dark brown. Some individual variation exists and some species show slightly lighter brown eyes in some individuals, but no raven species is documented to have characteristically blue adult eyes. Any apparently blue or pale eye in an adult raven is likely a photographic or lighting artefact, or a very young bird.' },
];

const verdict = 'partly-true'; // blue in juveniles, not in adults
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={path} ogType="article"
  datePublished="2026-11-07" dateModified="2026-11-07"
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} faq={faq} pageType="Article">

<article class="qa-page">

  <nav class="qa-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <ol>
      {breadcrumbs.map((b, i) => (
        <li>
          {i < breadcrumbs.length - 1
            ? <><a href={b.url}>{b.name}</a><span aria-hidden="true"> › </span></>
            : <span aria-current="page">{b.name}</span>}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Myths vs Facts · MYTH·11</p>
    <h1>Do Ravens Have Blue Eyes? Raven Eye Colour Explained</h1>
  </header>

  <div class="id-verdict verdict-partly-true" role="note" aria-label="Verdict">
    <strong>Verdict: Partly True</strong> — Juvenile ravens have blue-grey eyes. Adult ravens have dark brown eyes. The claim is true of young birds, false of adults.
  </div>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Adult Common Ravens have dark brown, near-black eyes. Juvenile ravens (from hatching to approximately one year of age) have blue-grey eyes, which darken progressively as melanin pigmentation develops in the iris. By full adult plumage (age 2–3), the eyes are uniformly dark brown. Claims of "blue-eyed ravens" likely refer to juvenile birds or to photographic artefacts.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Eye Colour by Age</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Eye colour in Common Ravens changes predictably with age:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Nestlings (0–8 weeks):</strong> eyes initially have pale, blue-grey irides — lacking adult melanin pigmentation.</li>
      <li><strong>Juveniles (2–12 months):</strong> eyes remain blue-grey to grey-brown, gradually darkening through the first year. This is a standard feature of juvenile corvid eye development.</li>
      <li><strong>Sub-adults (1–2 years):</strong> eyes approach adult colouration — brown, increasingly dark.</li>
      <li><strong>Adults (2+ years):</strong> eyes are uniformly dark brown, often appearing almost black in field conditions.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This ontogenetic (age-related) change in eye colour is shared with many other corvid species including American Crows, Carrion Crows, and Common Ravens throughout their range.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Why the Myth Persists</h2>
    <p>Several factors contribute to "blue-eyed raven" claims:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Juvenile birds:</strong> young ravens in their first year genuinely do have blue-grey eyes — people who encounter or photograph juvenile ravens may describe their eye colour accurately.</li>
      <li><strong>Photography:</strong> photographic flash and certain lighting conditions can make dark brown eyes appear lighter or bluer than they are.</li>
      <li><strong>Artwork and fiction:</strong> illustrations and fantasy representations of ravens frequently give them striking blue or violet eyes for dramatic effect — these depictions are not biologically accurate for adults.</li>
      <li><strong>Other species:</strong> some corvid relatives (magpies, some crows) have pale or yellow-green eyes in adults — these may be confused with ravens.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Pages</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/questions/what-does-a-raven-look-like/">What does a raven look like?</a></li>
      <li><a href="/questions/what-does-a-baby-raven-look-like/">What does a baby raven look like?</a></li>
      <li><a href="/species/common-raven/">Common Raven — species profile</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-faq qa-section" aria-labelledby="faq-h2">
    <h2 id="faq-h2">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    {faq.map(item => (
      <details class="qa-faq-item">
        <summary class="qa-faq-q">{item.q}</summary>
        <p class="qa-faq-a">{item.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="qa-sources qa-section" aria-labelledby="src-h2">
    <h2 id="src-h2">Sources</h2>
    <ol class="qa-src-list">
      <li id="src-1">Boarman, W.I. & Heinrich, B. (1999). Common Raven (<em>Corvus corax</em>). In <em>Birds of the World</em> (A.F. Poole, ed.). Cornell Lab of Ornithology. — plumage and eye colour by age class.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="qa-claim-note"><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> peer-reviewed study or field-guide data</p>
  </section>

</article>
</BaseLayout>

